WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:02.270
- Pets, they're just like people,

00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:04.860
except in any meaningful way

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:06.950
though the vast majority
of Americans have a pet.

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:08.130
According to a survey

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:10.080
from the American Pet
Products Association,

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.630
67% of U.S. households
have at least one pet.

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:15.680
This goes all the way to the White House.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:17.950
Some American presidents hate dogs

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:19.640
and would never have
one in the White House,

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.240
and other U.S. presidents

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.320
employ bloodthirsty killing
machines on White House grounds.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.320
And while we tend to
think of our fur babies

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:28.830
as our own children,

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:30.810
what rights do they really have?

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:31.730
Even though there's no rule

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:33.840
that says dogs can't play basketball,

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.870
could a cat inherit millions of dollars?

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:38.160
Could a dog run a Fortune 500 company?

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.240
Could a monkey own a copyright?

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.150
Or could an elephant run for office?

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:45.980
Well, the answer is it depends
because it always depends.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:48.160
But let's find out what
rights pets actually have

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:49.240
because this is America,

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:51.270
and you better believe
that this has wound up

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:52.680
in American courts before,

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:55.040
and it's going to end up in courts again.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:58.820
(contemplative music)

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:01.950
And perhaps the thing that
Americans love more than lawsuits

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:02.980
is their pets.

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:07.370
In 2019, Americans spent
a record $95.7 billion

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:09.100
on their pets, which makes sense.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:10.460
When it comes to our animal friends,

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:12.690
most of us would spare
no expense to ensure

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:14.770
that they're happy, healthy and pampered

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:16.800
like Meowxander Hamilton here.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.390
What happens if you want to make sure

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:21.210
that your Meowxander
Hamilton is taken care of

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:22.710
in the event of your untimely death?

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:25.420
Well, that actually raises some
interesting legal questions.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:26.570
Let's start with the basics.

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:28.850
While many of us may treat
our pets like people,

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:31.200
legally speaking, pets are
actually considered property

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:32.300
in the United States.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.117
As a result, animals
lack the legal ability,

00:01:34.117 --> 00:01:37.010
also known as capacity,
to enter into a contract.

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:39.320
Capacity refers to one's
demonstrated competence

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:40.620
to understand one's actions

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:42.670
and enter into a legally binding agreement

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:44.480
with another through mutual assent.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:46.630
And while we may think
that our pets understand us

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:48.820
when, after maybe one too many scotches,

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:50.250
we tell them about the challenges

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.800
of running your own law
practice and YouTube channel,

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.790
under the law, they do not.

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:57.080
And since animals are
property under the law,

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:58.530
they can't enter into contracts.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.140
That means that they
cannot legally receive

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:00.973
or own property.

00:02:00.973 --> 00:02:02.500
As a result, an owner's attempts

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:05.560
to directly leave property
to the pet will fail.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:07.810
In the case of In Re Estate of Russell,

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:09.470
a California court of appeals addressed

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:11.470
whether a pet owner can
legally divide her estate

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:14.860
between a close friend
and her dog, Roxy Russell.

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:16.990
And the trial court held
that despite the language

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:20.630
in the will directly leaving
property to Roxy the dog,

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:23.440
the owner actually intended
to leave her entire estate

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:25.550
to the close friend to care for the dog.

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:27.950
But the court of appeals
rejected this interpretation,

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:30.440
holding instead that the
will unambiguously sought

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:32.980
to divide the estate between
the friend and the dog.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:34.630
The court further rejected any argument

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:37.610
that the pet owner had created
trust to care for Roxy,

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:39.760
as the will did not
demonstrate a specific intent

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:42.830
to impose on the friend
the duty to care for Roxy.

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:45.960
Ruling that a dog cannot be
a direct will beneficiary,

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.810
the court concluded that the
gifts to Roxy were invalid.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:50.440
And accordingly, half
of the owner's estate

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:52.520
that had been intended for
Roxy as a matter of law

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:54.960
went to the owner's closest
relative, her niece.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:58.020
So to avoid problems like
the Russell case as of 2016,

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:01.780
all 50 states and DC have passed
laws regarding the creation

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:03.730
and use of so-called pet trusts.

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:05.840
A pet trust is a legal arrangement

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:08.280
that outlines how your
pet shall be provided for

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.070
and taken care of financially in the event

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:11.730
of your untimely death disability.

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:13.350
And in the vast majority of cases,

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:16.120
pet trusts and gifts
are non-controversial.

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:17.970
On occasion, they make international news,

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:19.850
especially when millions
of dollars are at stake.

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:22.580
Like for example, in
2007, when Leona Helmsley,

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:24.810
a New York billionaire
real estate developer,

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:28.670
left her Maltese, Trouble,
$12 million in a trust fund.

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:30.150
Known as the queen of mean

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:32.480
for her cervic personality
the way that she mistreated

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.370
her employees, Helmsley also
specifically disinherited

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:37.320
two of her own grandchildren in her will

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:41.000
quote for reasons which
are known to them, ouch.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:42.200
However, in 2008,

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:44.440
a New York judge slashed
Trouble's inheritance

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:46.800
from $12 million to $2
million on the grounds

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:50.000
that Helmsley was mentally
unfit when she'd made her will.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:50.833
As a result,

00:03:50.833 --> 00:03:51.910
the two disinherited grandchildren

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:53.600
received a total of six million.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.470
And despite her queen of mean reputation,

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:58.510
Helmsley clearly had a soft
spot for her dog Trouble

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:01.730
who died in 2011, presumably on a bed

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:03.680
of lots and lots of money.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:05.230
Though parenthetically,
that's not the only thing

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:07.150
that she had a soft spot for.

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:09.340
She also had a soft spot
for not paying taxes.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:11.300
Famously, she said we don't pay taxes,

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:13.350
only the little people pay taxes.

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:15.440
And in 1989, Helmsley was convicted

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:18.360
of federal tax income
evasion, and other crimes.

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:20.290
It turns out that not
all abrasive New York

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:22.550
real estate developers who
mistreat everyone around them

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:24.370
get away with not paying their taxes.

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:25.610
Though sentenced to 16 years,

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:27.140
she only served 19 months in prison

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:29.050
and two months of house arrest.

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:31.940
Though Helmsley was not alone
in leaving obscene amounts

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:33.250
of money to her pets.

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:35.100
In 2019, German fashion designer,

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:37.360
Karl Lagerfeld died and upon his death,

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.270
it was revealed that
Lagerfeld had left a chunk

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:42.530
of his near $300 million
fortune to Choupette,

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:43.610
a Burmese cat.

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:44.960
Though the pair lived in France,

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:46.760
which prohibits pets
from directly inheriting

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:48.940
their owner's property, under German law,

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:52.500
which governs Lagerfeld's will,
pets may inherit property.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:54.130
And in June 2020, it was reported

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:57.120
that not only were there seven
other human beneficiaries,

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:58.990
but none of the human errors

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:01.530
had been able to gain access
to their share of the estate.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:03.900
That's because Lagerfeld's
octogenarian accounts,

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:06.900
Lucien Frydlender, the only
person with enough knowledge

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:09.640
of Lagerfeld's vast to state
to aid in the distribution

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:12.330
to the beneficiary
mysteriously closed up shop

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:16.820
in September 2019 and has been
avoiding everyone's calls.

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:19.460
Frydlender's wife insisted
that her husband has not quote

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:20.920
run off to an island,

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:23.280
but is simply just too
sick to work on the case.

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:24.113
But of course,

00:05:24.113 --> 00:05:25.690
that definitely sounds
like the sort of thing

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:27.060
that someone who was hiding away

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:29.240
on a private island might say.

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:31.230
So while Lagerfeld's beneficiaries

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:32.680
fight for their inheritance,

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:34.210
Choupette does not even need the money.

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:37.080
The famous feline who has
hundreds of thousands of followers

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:39.990
on Instagram already earns
millions of euros each year

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:43.130
by appearing in advertising
campaigns on magazine covers

00:05:43.130 --> 00:05:46.340
and even has her own wine
brand and makeup line.

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:49.690
She's basically the cat
equivalent of the Kardashians.

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:51.720
Clearly I'm in the wrong
business as a lawyer

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:54.070
and should have pursued a lucrative career

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:56.840
as an international
multimillionaire cat model.

00:05:56.840 --> 00:05:58.640
So yeah, I guess I'm
saying if anyone needs

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:01.860
a YouTube bird model, give me a call.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.870
But the past is the past,

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:06.140
speaking of which, remember in 2019

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:10.300
when a banana taped to a
wall sold for $120,000?

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:12.810
If you found yourself asking
did a monkey make this,

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:14.250
well, not this time,

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:16.410
but there is plenty of
animal made art out there

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:18.490
like Pockets Warhol, a Capuchin monkey

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:20.230
who's been painting since 2011

00:06:20.230 --> 00:06:22.100
and then there's Lucky the rescue elephant

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:25.120
painting in Cambodia and
while seeing animals make art

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:27.520
is fun, it raises an interesting question.

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:30.590
Do these animals actually
own the copyright

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:31.950
in the works that they create?

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:34.660
I've covered copyright
law in previous videos,

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:36.220
but to summarize briefly,

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:38.230
copyright extends legal
protection to authors

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:39.390
of any original work fixed

00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:40.608
in a tangible medium of expression

00:06:40.608 --> 00:06:42.590
such as just painting songs, novels,

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:43.770
and even photographs.

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:46.440
The holder of a copyright
owns a set of exclusive rights

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:48.170
to make and sell copies of their works,

00:06:48.170 --> 00:06:50.340
create every works and perform

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:51.730
and display their works publicly.

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:54.310
Now the issue whether
animals own the copyright

00:06:54.310 --> 00:06:56.840
in their artistic creations
were at the forefront

00:06:56.840 --> 00:06:59.720
of what became known as
the monkey selfie case.

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:02.630
See back in 2011, a macaque
monkey named Naruto.

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:04.810
I assume that's Naruto as in the anime

00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:06.830
and not as in Naruto as in Pablo,

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:08.710
but using a camera strategically placed

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:11.530
by British nature
photographer, David John Slater

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:13.310
while on assignment in Indonesia

00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:15.490
famously took a monkey selfie.

00:07:15.490 --> 00:07:18.260
Slater included Naruto's
photos in a book he published.

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:21.010
These photos subsequently
went viral and in 2015,

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:23.380
the People for the Ethical
Treatment of Animals or PETA

00:07:23.380 --> 00:07:25.430
known for their attention
grabbing protests

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:27.830
that has made them the
frequent target for parody

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:31.210
sued Slater and the self
publishing company blurb

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:32.630
on Naruto's behalf.

00:07:32.630 --> 00:07:35.323
- The outside world looks down
on a man marrying a llama,

00:07:35.323 --> 00:07:37.853
but our love knows no boundaries.

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:41.350
- Wow, you guys really love animals.

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:44.340
- PETA argued that publishing
and selling the photographs

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:48.050
infringed on Naruto's rights
under the U.S. copyright act.

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:50.900
And PETA sought a judgment
affirming Naruto's copyright

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:52.464
in the photos and requested that PETA

00:07:52.464 --> 00:07:55.630
be appointed to administer
all proceeds from the photos

00:07:55.630 --> 00:07:57.710
for the endangered species benefit.

00:07:57.710 --> 00:07:59.920
The defendants countered that as a monkey,

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:01.630
Naruto could not own a copyright.

00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:02.690
And while the case was pending,

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:04.900
numerous copyright experts
in both the U.S. and UK

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:07.550
argued that Slater's role
in the photographic process

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:10.610
and his artistic choices
would be sufficient

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:13.070
to grant him a copyright
in the photographs,

00:08:13.070 --> 00:08:15.260
though a formal court
judgment might be necessary

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:16.650
to be determinative.

00:08:16.650 --> 00:08:18.620
In fact, in a December 14 publication,

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:20.400
the United States Copyright Office stated

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:23.050
that quote to qualify
as a work of authorship,

00:08:23.050 --> 00:08:25.530
a work must be created by a human being,

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:27.930
specifically stating that a
photograph taken by a monkey

00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:29.910
or a mural painted by an animal

00:08:29.910 --> 00:08:31.930
would not receive copyright protection.

00:08:31.930 --> 00:08:33.510
And in January 2016,

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:34.920
the U.S. district court agreed

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:36.760
with district court judge William Orrick

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:39.040
writing while Congress and the president

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:41.600
can extend the protection
of law to animals

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:42.710
as well as humans,

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:44.820
there is no indication that they did so

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:46.150
in the Copyright Act.

00:08:46.150 --> 00:08:48.020
PETA appealed to the ninth
circuit court of appeals

00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:49.320
and in September 2017,

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:51.630
both PETA and the photographer
agreed to a settlement

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:54.900
in which Slater would donate
25% of any future revenue

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:57.480
to charities that protect
the macaque habitats.

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:00.450
Unfortunately for Slater,
the damage was already done.

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:02.670
The litigation had been
financially devastating to him,

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:04.790
leaving him broke and
unable to pay his attorney

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:07.110
or even replace the
broken camera equipment.

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:09.780
Now after settling, PETA
attended to dismiss the case,

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:12.530
but the ninth circuit refused
stating that it wanted to set

00:09:12.530 --> 00:09:14.270
a legal precedent for similar cases

00:09:14.270 --> 00:09:15.660
that would arise in the future.

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:17.920
The panel also ripped the
animal welfare organization

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:20.180
for what it viewed as PETA
attempting to manipulate

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:23.250
the legal precedent in a way
that suits their own interests.

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:25.930
And in 2018, the ninth
circuit did set a precedent,

00:09:25.930 --> 00:09:27.660
unanimously ruling that while humans

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:30.700
can file copyright
lawsuits, animals may not.

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:32.810
And as a result, the court
concluded that Naruto

00:09:32.810 --> 00:09:35.170
did not own any copyright in the photos.

00:09:35.170 --> 00:09:37.670
Now, the three judge
panel also excoriated PETA

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:38.760
for its litigation tactics,

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:40.800
accusing the organization of using Naruto

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:43.540
as a tool for its own ideological agenda

00:09:43.540 --> 00:09:45.470
while pretending to be a friend.

00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:46.560
PETA appears to have failed

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:48.480
to live up to the title of friend.

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:50.790
In the wake of PETA's proposed dismissal,

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:52.370
Naruto is left without an advocate,

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:54.470
his supposed friend
having abandoned Naruto's

00:09:54.470 --> 00:09:56.130
substantive claims and what appears

00:09:56.130 --> 00:09:58.260
to be an effort to prevent
the publication of a decision

00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:00.380
adverse to PETA's institutional interests.

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:02.330
Were he capable of
recognizing this abandonment,

00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:04.730
we wonder whether Naruto
might initiate an action

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:06.200
for breach of confidential relationship

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:09.490
against his former next
friend PETA for its failure

00:10:09.490 --> 00:10:11.800
to pursue his interests before its own.

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:13.360
Puzzlingly, while
representing to the world

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:15.240
that animals are not ours to eat, wear,

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:17.050
experiment on, use for entertainment

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:18.850
or abuse in any other way,

00:10:18.850 --> 00:10:21.910
PETA seems to employ
Naruto as an unwitting pawn

00:10:21.910 --> 00:10:23.560
in its ideological goals.

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:24.393
So there you have it.

00:10:24.393 --> 00:10:26.220
The ninth circuit says that currently,

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:28.400
the Copyright Act does
not apply to animals,

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:30.350
but it could be extended to do so

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:34.130
and it accuses PETA of
treating animals unethically,

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:35.920
but that's just silly copyright issues.

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:38.220
What about animals holding real power?

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:40.190
Well, there've been
countless movies and TV shows

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:41.600
over the years with the exact same story

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:43.400
of down on their luck sports team

00:10:43.400 --> 00:10:44.700
immediately changes their fortune

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:46.610
by substituting the team's animal mascot.

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:47.770
And they all seem to get around

00:10:47.770 --> 00:10:50.340
the very, very obvious objections

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:52.470
such a substitution in the exact same way.

00:10:52.470 --> 00:10:54.440
Well, show me in the
rule book where it says

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:57.610
that we can't substitute
our animal mascot of choice

00:10:57.610 --> 00:11:00.400
who happens to be magically
good at this particular sport

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:03.100
into our particular sports ball team.

00:11:03.100 --> 00:11:04.300
Show me in the rules.

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:05.980
- He's right, ain't no rules

00:11:05.980 --> 00:11:07.863
that the dog can't play basketball.

00:11:09.470 --> 00:11:12.370
- Lawyered, just don't look
at the Copyright Act, I guess.

00:11:13.450 --> 00:11:14.350
But in the real world,

00:11:14.350 --> 00:11:16.410
although there's no specific
rule against substituting

00:11:16.410 --> 00:11:19.560
the entire Chicago bears
lineup with a sleuth of bears,

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:22.590
yes that is the actual group
name for a group of bears,

00:11:22.590 --> 00:11:25.280
in part because the bears
would always be offsides

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:26.240
and not for nothing,

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:28.810
they would probably rip the
opposing team to shreds,

00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:30.850
but in the late 19th century,

00:11:30.850 --> 00:11:33.080
the political term the yellow dog Democrat

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:35.420
described to Southern voters
who allegedly would vote

00:11:35.420 --> 00:11:37.800
for a yellow dog before any Republican.

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:40.140
Now you might be surprised
to know that many voters

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:42.860
in U.S. history have followed that mantra

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:45.140
by electing animals to local office.

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:46.210
Now generally speaking,

00:11:46.210 --> 00:11:48.540
election law regulations would
disqualify animal candidates

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:51.050
by explicitly requiring
candidates just to be human

00:11:51.050 --> 00:11:52.950
or perhaps by requiring candidates

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:56.760
to at least perform tasks
that only humans can perform

00:11:56.760 --> 00:11:58.970
such as sign their names legibly.

00:11:58.970 --> 00:12:02.650
However, when the law is silent
or ambiguous on the matter,

00:12:02.650 --> 00:12:05.300
certain localities, usually
unincorporated towns,

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:06.980
have allowed animal office holders

00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:08.980
under the same logic of air bud.

00:12:08.980 --> 00:12:11.820
Well, there's nothing in the election code

00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:13.900
that says we can't have a dog mayor

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:16.650
so let's do that
apparently for some reason.

00:12:16.650 --> 00:12:18.720
And wouldn't you know it in 1938,

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:20.600
Boston Curtis, a brown mule,

00:12:20.600 --> 00:12:22.340
was put forward as a candidate for

00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:23.830
a Republican precinct committee man

00:12:23.830 --> 00:12:26.000
in the small town of Milton Washington.

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:28.280
Boston was nominated
by the democratic mayor

00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:30.210
Ken Asimons as a prank for two reasons.

00:12:30.210 --> 00:12:32.800
One, he wanted to embarrass
the Republican party

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:35.550
and he wanted to show the
flaws of the primary system

00:12:35.550 --> 00:12:38.610
in which voters quote often
have no idea whom they support.

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:39.870
The mayor would be proven correct

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:41.060
because humans are stupid.

00:12:41.060 --> 00:12:43.080
Boston was elected with 51% of the vote,

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:45.510
despite not offering a
platform or making a speech

00:12:45.510 --> 00:12:49.420
and being known as someone
that was stubborn as a mule

00:12:49.420 --> 00:12:52.000
and in the unincorporated
town of Lajitas, Texas,

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:53.470
they live up to the state's reputation

00:12:53.470 --> 00:12:54.930
for demanding limited government.

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:55.850
Since the 1980s,

00:12:55.850 --> 00:12:57.630
the town has consistently elected members

00:12:57.630 --> 00:13:00.710
of one political dynasty, Clary Henry,

00:13:00.710 --> 00:13:01.900
the beer drinking goat.

00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:03.110
I mean, just look at him,

00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:05.570
there's a kid you could
really grab a beer with.

00:13:05.570 --> 00:13:08.610
Now, this saga began when
a man who had the audacity

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:10.830
to be from Houston
decided to run for mayor

00:13:10.830 --> 00:13:12.610
and an outraged local businessman

00:13:12.610 --> 00:13:14.830
challenged him with a goat.

00:13:14.830 --> 00:13:16.610
Though the Houstonian won that election,

00:13:16.610 --> 00:13:19.650
Clay Henry won the next one
in a landslide and since then,

00:13:19.650 --> 00:13:21.480
every Clay Henry has been elected.

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:23.090
Today, the town is actually
under the leadership

00:13:23.090 --> 00:13:25.030
of Clay Henry the fourth,

00:13:25.030 --> 00:13:27.030
but when it comes to
electing animals to office

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:28.130
in the United States,

00:13:28.130 --> 00:13:30.610
dogs remained by far
the most popular choice.

00:13:30.610 --> 00:13:34.050
In 1981, Roscoe, a black
Labrador Rottweiler mix,

00:13:34.050 --> 00:13:36.760
defeated two humans to become
mayor of Sonoma, California.

00:13:36.760 --> 00:13:39.480
He of course ran as a
Republican on an agenda

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:40.870
of a bone in every dish,

00:13:40.870 --> 00:13:44.350
a cat in every tree and a
fire hydrant on every corner.

00:13:44.350 --> 00:13:46.820
He served his community for
13 years until his death.

00:13:46.820 --> 00:13:49.230
And there's actually a
statute in his honor.

00:13:49.230 --> 00:13:52.260
Similarly, Idyllwild California
is an unincorporated town

00:13:52.260 --> 00:13:54.870
and has no local
government, but since 2012,

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:57.640
the town has modeled its
election as a fundraiser

00:13:57.640 --> 00:13:58.473
for the benefit

00:13:58.473 --> 00:14:00.920
of a local animal rescue
nonprofit organization.

00:14:00.920 --> 00:14:03.780
Locals would nominate their
dogs and cats to run for mayor.

00:14:03.780 --> 00:14:07.000
14 dogs and two cats ran in
the first election and Max,

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:09.520
a golden retriever, was
elected as its first mayor.

00:14:09.520 --> 00:14:12.410
When Mayor Max crossed the
Rainbow Bridge in 2013,

00:14:12.410 --> 00:14:14.450
he was succeeded by
another golden retriever,

00:14:14.450 --> 00:14:16.840
Maximus Mighty Dog Mueller the second

00:14:16.840 --> 00:14:18.510
or Mighty Max for short

00:14:18.510 --> 00:14:20.470
who still leads the town to this day.

00:14:20.470 --> 00:14:23.580
His oath reads I, Max,
do swear on my food bowl

00:14:23.580 --> 00:14:26.760
to faithfully execute my
duties as mayor of Idyllwild.

00:14:26.760 --> 00:14:28.810
The mayor's duties include
meeting with the constituents

00:14:28.810 --> 00:14:29.710
in his pickup truck,

00:14:29.710 --> 00:14:31.980
attending city events and
making a variety of appearances

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:34.240
on state and local media
to promote the town.

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:35.073
But of course,

00:14:35.073 --> 00:14:36.790
